1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT Officer, Member of Parliament Johan Printz
2 CONT
2 CONT Member of Parliament Johan Printz (1592-1663) was, according to his contemporaries, an unusually tall, rugged and regal man. He served as a lieutenant in Gustav Horn's regiment in 1624, then as a lieutenant in Åke Totti's cuirassi
2 CONC er company. Cuirassiers were armored cavalry. Totti had both a provincial company and a cuirassier company at the same time, both Finnish. Johan Printz then served as the cavalry master of the Turku County cavalry in 1630, as a ma
2 CONC jor in 1634 and as a lieutenant colonel in 1636. He was the commander of the Ostrobothnian company of the Turku County cavalry regiment, which was still in Elbing in 1635, then moved to Germany via Pomerania. Johan Printz was take
2 CONC n prisoner by Halle. After his release, he transferred to the West Götaland cavalry regiment in 1638. His military career ended in the defense of the city of Chemnitz in 1640, and he was court-martialed in Stockholm due to the sur
2 CONC render of the city. However, he was given a noble title and moved to Delaware, America, where he served as governor of the Swedish colony of New Sweden in 1642-1653. He then became a state councilor and governor. (Matti J. Kankaan
2 CONC pää: Ostrobothnia cavalry regiment in the 1620s and 1630s. Southern Pohjolaiset Juuret 2/2006, KTMP Group Mustasaari 2016, pp.43-50)
2 CONT
2 CONT Riding list from 1633, Vähäkyrö, houses that equipped soldiers in Major Johan Printz's company:
2 CONT
