2 CONT '''All these mortgages were signed by GundeRosenkrantz, & Mrs. (PernillelRosenkrantz) and their son Jakob Rosenkrantz.''' The mortgages were confirmed by the King on 22 November 1667. Jergen Rosenkrantz's above-mentioned letter to
2 CONC  the King dated 17 July 1666 begins thus: "Your Royal Majesty's usual clemency gives me the courage, my most gracious Lord and King, to reveal my condition, which my unfortunate brother has put me in, which [o: my condition] is no
2 CONC w certainly more to be regretted than described, and therefore most to be regretted, because I have suffered so well, and must now suffer so badly because of him, and therefore not to be described, because I may be ashamed of this
2 CONC  distress, which I am thereby forced to reveal to anyone."e In order to understand this outburst by Jergen Rosenkrantz, it is It is necessary to remember that Gunde Rosenkrantz, in his declaration of 28 December 1662, promised his
2 CONC  creditors that, when his conditions improved, 'by whatever means it might be, whether purchase, donation, trade or inheritance, then to pay and make good all the damage that they might suffer from me.
