2 CONT Johan Carl pleads for a service in the army, Johan Carl 1692 appeal in a letter to the Governor General for a service within the army to relieve their poverty. I quote parts of the letter: "So highly Eder's welding beprijseliga ba
2 CONC rmhertigheet Excell to hielpa all nödlijdande, as my outsägeliga armodh and poverty, Otl: r me hopetals will, for using reductioner, and other olykeliga tillfall, förordsaka my big dristigheet, emädan altsedan no tienst been iag w
2 CONC ar reached, oansedt iag ödmiukel. " The letter ended: "The grace that me bevijses, iag my poor wife and children, with our hiertans tears for Esther Excell's stedse prijsa hoos of the Pope and in to my dödh förblif: (a)
2 CONT From castles to Hut 1693 was the new owner of Yaböke. As is apparent from the letter above was Karl XI's reduction the reason. Karl XI's reduction started in 1681 and continued throughout the 1680-1690 century. On several occasion
2 CONC s letters refer to a military reduction jordebok from 1652 and Yaböke previously mentioned a donation towards Maria Catharina's grandfather, Per Jonsson Gyllensvärd. Probably settled Johan Carl, his wife and the youngest children,
2 CONC  Christina then 15 years old, as a sponsor for Pax child at Yaböke. 1709, Johan Carl with his wife and younger children lived in Femsjö when he bought the farm Lilla Duvhult. Timing fits well with what was written in the book abou
2 CONC t old Steneby: "half the estate Haque with underlying torp, who Blessed Virgin Mary Bullock, the former owner and after her death benefited her sister Virgin Margaret Bullock, which likewise Mr. Johan Carl Rosenschantz and his wif
2 CONC e Maria Catarina Svinhufvud for adequate payment waived all their supposed pretention and solution right according to their writings of 16 July and 20 August 1708. "As it is apparent from the letter to De la Gardie, there were man
2 CONC y children in the family, 7-8, including one daughter died in infancy. I followed Christina,because she was Carl Bengtsson’s mother.  Johan Carl was not many years on Small Duvhult. He died at the age of 69 on 12 January 1713 and 
2 CONC was buried on 25 May on the south wall of the Church, just like with Sal Priest Nastadius grave in the East (between the two Windows on the south wall of the Church)".
